      <floats-group id="article-32402.s12">
        <fig id="article-32402.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Ligamentous Anatomy of the Left Elbow, Anterior View. This illustration shows the anterior and medial aspects of the left elbow joint, highlighting key bones and ligamentous structures. Osseous landmarks include the distal humerus (medial epicondyle, trochlea, capitellum), proximal ulna (olecranon, coronoid process, radial notch), and proximal radius (radial head, radial tuberosity). Ligaments depicted are the anterior ligament, ulnar collateral ligament with anterior, posterior, and transverse bands, annular ligament encircling the radial head, and the oblique cord spanning from the ulna to the radius. Capsular attachments and brachialis origin are also shown. The 3 apertures in the inferior portion represent passage for dorsal interosseous vessels. Henry Vandyke Carter, <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?search=gray+plate+329&#x00026;title=Special:MediaSearch&#x00026;go=Go&#x00026;type=image">Public Domain</ext-link>, via Wikimedia Commons</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Gray329" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-32402.s13">
        <fig id="article-32402.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Elbow Joint Structures. Panel (a) displays the distal humerus, proximal ulna, and proximal radius, showing the articulation at the humeroulnar and humeroradial joints. Key landmarks such as the trochlea, capitulum, olecranon, and coronoid process are labeled, highlighting the elbow&#x02019;s major osseous features. Panel (b) illustrates the principal stabilizing ligaments of the elbow: the ulnar collateral ligament (with anterior, posterior, and transverse bundles), radial collateral ligament, and the annular ligament, which encircles the radial head and secures the proximal radioulnar joint. Panel&#x000a0;(c) highlights muscle groups acting at the elbow, including the biceps brachii and brachialis (anterior/flexor compartment) and triceps brachii (posterior/extensor compartment), demonstrating their origins and insertions on the humerus, radius, and ulna. OpenStax College, <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:915_Elbow_Joint.jpg">Public Domain</ext-link>, via Wikimedia Commons</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="915_Elbow_Joint" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-32402.s14">
        <fig id="article-32402.image.f3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Ligamentous Structures of the Elbow. This illustration depicts the ligamentous anatomy of the elbow in multiple views, highlighting the medial and lateral stabilizing complexes. Labeled structures include the lateral radial collateral ligament (LRCL), annular ligament (AL), medial collateral ligament complex (MCLC), lateral ulnar collateral ligament (LUCL), accessory lateral ligament, anterior medial collateral ligament (AMCL), posterior medial collateral ligament (PMCL), and the Cooper ligament. Illustration by Katherine Humphreys and Frances Humphreys</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Elbow" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-32402.s15">
        <fig id="article-32402.image.f4" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Ulnar Collateral Ligament and Ulnar Nerve. This diagram depicts the medial aspect of the elbow, highlighting the 3 bundles of the ulnar collateral ligament (anterior, posterior, and transverse) and the annular ligament. The course of the ulnar nerve is illustrated in yellow, showing its proximity to the ulnar collateral ligament and its passage posterior to the medial epicondyle. Contributed by&#x000a0;S Bhimji, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Medial__elbow" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-32402.s16">
        <fig id="article-32402.image.f5" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Osborne Ligament in the Cubital Tunnel. Dissection image showing the Osborne ligament spanning between the medial epicondyle of the humerus and the olecranon of the ulna, forming the roof of the cubital tunnel, which houses the ulnar nerve. Contributed by S Bhimji, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Osborne__band" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-32402.s17">
        <fig id="article-32402.image.f6" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Elbow Joint Arterial Anastomosis. The blood vessels contributing to this network include the anterior profunda brachii, superior ulnar collateral, brachial, inferior ulnar collateral, anterior ulnar recurrent, posterior ulnar recurrent, common interosseous, dorsal interosseous, volar interosseous, radial, recurrent interosseous, and radial recurrent arteries. The anterior and radial collateral branches of the profunda brachii also connect to this network. Henry Vandyke Carter, <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?search=Gray+Anatomy+526&#x00026;title=Special%3AMediaSearch&#x00026;type=image">Public Domain,</ext-link> via Wikimedia Commons</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Gray526" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-32402.s18">
        <fig id="article-32402.image.f7" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Nerves and Blood Vessels of the Arm and Forearm, Anterior View. This anatomical illustration depicts the nerves and arteries of the upper limb, highlighting the brachial artery (in red) running along the arm and forearm, with its major branches including the deep brachial artery, radial artery, and ulnar artery. Key nerves include the musculocutaneous, median, radial (with deep and superficial branches), and ulnar nerves, as well as the medial antebrachial cutaneous and dorsal branch of the ulnar nerve. The course of these nerves illustrates their anatomical relations with muscles such as the biceps brachii and brachialis and their distribution into the hand and digits. Henry Vandyke Carter, <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?search=Gray+Anatomy+816&#x00026;title=Special%3AMediaSearch&#x00026;type=image">Public Domain</ext-link>, via Wikimedia Commons</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Gray816" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
